  count), and `plinking_pgen_io := 'localize'` (it copies every `.pgen` at bind).

### 10.7 Measured (2026-11-10): a `WHERE` on `IID` prunes the sample axis

In genotype orient a row is one variant × one sample, so a `WHERE` on `IID` removes whole sample
blocks. `PfilePushdownComplexFilter` now also collects `IID = c` and `IID IN (c, …)` from the
conjunction. `PruneSamplesByIid` narrows `sample_indices` to the samples with those IIDs,
intersected with `samples :=`. A sample block with no selected sample is then never opened:
`ConcatPgenReader::Open` skips it, and `CheckDeferredPgenHeaders` skips its header too. Every
filter stays in place, so the rows equal a full read.

Same fixture as §10.5 (10 × 4 grid, 30000 variants × 10000 samples), genotype orient, 4 threads,
3 runs per case. The two builds are main at 9166930 and this change.

| Case | `.pgen` distinct / opens, before | after | time, before | after |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `WHERE IID = 'S02501'` | 40 / 200 | 10 / 50 | 11.50 s | 0.12 s |
| `WHERE IID IN (2 blocks)` | 40 / 200 | 20 / 100 | 12.17 s | 0.10 s |
| `WHERE IID = … AND CHROM = '3'` | 4 / 20 | 1 / 5 | 2.02 s | 0.09 s |
| `WHERE IID = 'NOPE'` | 40 / 200 | 10 / 50 | 11.45 s | 0.11 s |
| `samples := ['S02501']` | 40 / 80 | 10 / 50 | 0.09 s | 0.09 s |
| `WHERE hash(IID) = hash(…)` (not recognized) | 40 / 200 | 40 / 200 | 12.24 s | 12.24 s |
| sample orient, `WHERE IID = …` | 40 / 80 | 40 / 80 | 2.50 s | 2.55 s |

- In each case, `count(*)` and `sum(genotype)` are the same before and after.
- Inferred, not profiled: most of the time saved is not I/O. Before, the scan emitted 300M rows
  (30000 × 10000), and the `WHERE` removed all but 30000. Now it emits one sample per variant.
- `samples :=` already skipped the data reads; the header check opened the other blocks. Now
  it does not.
- **Not with `af_range` / `ac_range`.** They compute each variant's frequency over the sample
  set, before the `WHERE`. A narrowed set would change which variants pass, so they keep it whole.
- An IID that no sample has keeps one sample to scan; the `WHERE` then removes its rows.
- A duplicate IID (`concatenate` does not require unique IIDs) keeps every sample that has it.
- **Not done: sample orient.** It reads the whole matrix at bind, before the planner calls the
  hook. This is the `c.genotypes_by_sample WHERE IID = …` case. To prune it, the matrix read must
  move from bind to InitGlobal. That is a separate change.
- Variant orient has no `IID` column. A sample filter there is `samples :=`.
- A plain `combine_samples := 'concatenate'` / `'disjoint'` list prunes the same way, because the
  gate is `IsConcat()`, not "from a catalog". There the scan skips the data reads, but bind still
  opens every shard's `.pgen` header: stage 5 defers it only for a catalog.
- A parquet `.psam` works when the catalog template names the `.psam.parquet`. A template that
  names `samples.psam` reads the text file, even with a parquet companion beside it.
- Side effect on `samples :=`: before, a count mismatch in an unselected sample block made the
  read fail. Now that block is not opened, so the error shows only for a query that selects one of
  its samples. This is the stage 5 rule ("a cell the query does not read is not checked").
- Test: `test/sql/read_pfile_catalog_iid_prune.test`. Its fixture has a garbage `.pgen` in every
  cell of one sample block.

### 10.8 Measured (2026-11-11): bind no longer checks that a cell's files exist

Bind ran path discovery on each catalog cell: `ResolveExistingPath` on the `.pgen`, `.pvar` and
