### 10.10 Measured (2026-11-11): the IID prune in sample orient, per-element modes

The per-element modes (array, list, struct, columns, also with `phased` or `dosages`) read every
genotype into a matrix. Bind did that fill, before the planner calls the hook, so a `WHERE` on `IID`
could not reduce it. The fill is now `FillSampleMatrix`. For a catalog read with no filter parameter
(`smp_headers_at_init`, now for every genotypes mode), `EnsureLazyVariants` calls it at InitGlobal,
after the header checks. The matrix then holds only the samples that the hook kept. With a filter
parameter, bind still fills the matrix, as before: the count filter decides the variants over every
sample at bind, so every cell is open anyway.

Three behaviours move from bind to InitGlobal, for a catalog read with no filter parameter only:

- `DESCRIBE` opens no `.pgen`. A bad or mismatched cell shows when the query runs. A missing cell
  `.pvar` still errors at bind, and it is now the file the error names (the `.pgen` was first before).
- **`plinking_max_matrix_elements` applies to the pruned matrix.** A `WHERE IID` that keeps one
  sample of a cohort too large for the limit now runs. The check still runs before the fill
  allocates. A `DESCRIBE` of a read over the limit no longer errors. A plain (non-catalog) read
  keeps the check at bind.
- A prepared statement fills the matrix once (the `lazy_variants_loaded` flag), from the narrowed
  sample set.

