### 10.11 Measured (2026-11-11): the §5 spike — the autosomal count kernel is already reused; the surface is not

**Question (§5).** Can the cascade reader reuse the `plink_freq` / `missing` / `hardy` count kernels
on a projection, rather than add a new count path?

**Answer: for autosomes, yes, and it already does.** `read_pfile(genotypes := 'counts' | 'stats')` in variant
orient calls `PgrGetCounts` per variant (`pfile_reader.cpp`, the aggregate branch of the scan). A
catalog read goes through `ConcatPgenReader::GetCounts` (`pgen_concat_reader.cpp:160`), which sums
`PgrGetCounts` over the sample blocks of each variant. That is the kernel `plink_hardy` and
`plink_freq` use for autosomes. No decode happens on this path.

**It is not the whole `plink_*` kernel.** On chrX non-PAR, chrY and chrMT, `plink_freq`,
`plink_hardy` and `plink_missing` decode the calls and call `ComputeSexAwareCounts` with a sex
array (`plink_common.hpp:882`). The `counts` / `stats` modes call raw `PgrGetCounts` for every
variant, so their numbers differ there. The `plink_*` functions also differ from each other on
chrX: `plink_hardy` counts the female stratum, `plink_freq` counts every included sample, with a
haploid call as homozygous. The `wes_chr10` oracle below has no sex chromosome, so it cannot show
either difference.

Fixture: the §10.5 grid (10 × 4, 30000 variants × 10000 samples) and the bare `wes_chr10` it was cut
from. The build is 8416484 (same reader as main at e375536). 4 threads, 5 runs each, wall time.

| Read | wall (s) |
|---|---|
| grid catalog, `genotypes := 'counts'`, `sum(genotypes.het)` | 0.10–0.15 |
| grid catalog, `genotypes := 'stats'`, `sum(genotypes.af)` | 0.10–0.11 |
| grid catalog, default array, het count by `list_filter` | 1.24–1.26 |
| bare `wes_chr10`, `counts` | 0.09–0.10 |
| bare `wes_chr10`, `plink_hardy` / `plink_freq` | 0.09–0.11 |
| bare `wes_chr10`, default array, het count by `list_filter` | 1.13–1.24 |

- **Correctness.** The catalog `stats` read equals `plink_hardy` + `plink_freq(counts := true)` on
  the bare fileset, joined on `ID`: hom_ref, het, hom_alt, missing and af (to 1e-12) agree on all
  30000 variants, 0 mismatches. As for `plink_*`, `PgrGetCounts` buckets REF against any ALT, so a
  multiallelic variant collapses to one biallelic figure.
- **Projection through a view works.** On `ATTACH … (TYPE pfile_catalog)`,
  `SELECT count(POS) FROM g.genotypes` took 0.10 s against 0.25–0.27 s for
  `count(genotypes[1])` (3 runs each). So an unprojected `genotypes` column is not decoded through
  the view.
- **Outliers I could not explain.** Three first runs in a fresh Slurm job took far longer: 71.5 s
  (grid `counts`), 24.1 s (ATTACH `count(POS)`) and 2.27 s (bare `counts`). None reproduced in the
  next runs or the 5-run series. I (Claude) infer a cold cache or host load; I did not find the cause.

**The gap is the surface.** `c.genotypes` is `SELECT * FROM read_pfile(catalog := …)`
(`pfile_catalog_storage.cpp:316`), the default array mode. A view cannot pass `genotypes :=`, so a
count derived from `c.genotypes` decodes every genotype (about 12× the counts path above). §5's
fix is scalar count columns (`HOM_REF_CT`, `HET_CT`, `HOM_ALT_CT`, `MISSING_CT`, `OBS_CT`,
`ALT_FREQ`, `F_MISS`, named as `plink_*` emits them) that the scan computes only when projected.
Today one `read_pfile` call emits either the `genotypes` decode or the counts struct, never both.
Where those columns live is an owner decision: on every variant-orient `read_pfile` (changes
`SELECT *`), behind a parameter that the ATTACH view passes, or as a separate view over
`genotypes := 'stats'` (no C++).

A cost of the first form: the pfile writer copies each extra input column into the `.pvar` under
its own name (`docs/functions/write_pfile.md`, extra columns). So `COPY (SELECT * FROM read_pfile(…))
TO … (FORMAT pfile)` would write the count columns into the output `.pvar`. Those counts go stale
after a sample subset.

**Owner decision (2026-11-11).** Teague chose the second form: a `read_pfile` parameter adds the
count columns, and the ATTACH `c.genotypes` view passes it. Plain `read_pfile` output is unchanged.

### 10.12 Measured (2026-11-11): `freq := true` and the `c.genotypes` count columns

`read_pfile(…, freq := true)` adds `plink_freq(counts := true)`'s value columns (`ALT_FREQ`, `OBS_CT`,
`HOM_REF_CT`, `HET_CT`, `HOM_ALT_CT`, `MISSING_CT`) in variant orient. The `c.genotypes` view passes
it for every kind. The rule is parity: each column holds `plink_freq`'s value for the same variant
and samples. So it uses `plink_freq`'s two paths — `PgrGetCounts` (or `ConcatPgenReader::GetCounts`)
on autosomes and PAR, decode + `ComputeSexAwareCounts` on chrX non-PAR / chrY / chrMT — with SEX
from the `.psam` and the PAR bounds from `build :=` (default GRCh38, as in `plink_freq`).

- **Name set.** The `plink_*` functions do not agree on every name: `OBS_CT` counts alleles in
  `plink_freq` and genotypes in `plink_missing`, and `plink_hardy`'s chrX counts are the female
  stratum. So the columns are exactly one function's set, `plink_freq`'s. `F_MISS` is left out.
- **Projection.** A column is computed only when it is in `column_ids`, so a `WHERE` on a count
  column with only `ID` selected still computes it. The pushed filter runs in the generic residual
  applicator.
- **Errors.** Genotype or sample orient, `dosages := true` (plink_freq's dosage mode weights by
  dosage), `build :=` without `freq`, and a `genotypes := 'columns'` IID with a freq column's name.
- **COPY.** The writer keeps extra input columns, so `COPY (SELECT * FROM c.genotypes) TO …
  (FORMAT pfile)` writes the six columns into the `.pvar`. Documented in `pfile_catalog.md`; not
  refused.

